  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a battery electrolyte concept based on liquefied gaseous solvents. Two battery cells are shown with swirling vapor-like regions above them, indicating electrolyte species in a gas/liquid equilibrium. The left side includes labeled molecular components such as TFE, Me₂O, FSI⁻, and PFE, suggesting the formulation of a mixed liquefied gaseous electrolyte. In the center, arrows and a recycling symbol emphasize that these gaseous solvents are recyclable, implying a reversible or recoverable electrolyte system rather than a conventional permanent liquid.\n\nThe graphic also highlights two key advantages. A crossed-out flame icon indicates the electrolyte is non-flammable, pointing to improved safety. At the bottom, a schematic of a lithium-metal surface labeled “F-rich SEI” shows that the electrolyte forms a fluorine-rich solid electrolyte interphase on Li metal. This implies a protective interphase that supports lithium-metal battery operation.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that liquefied gaseous electrolytes can be recovered and reused, while also offering safety benefits and favorable interfacial chemistry for lithium metal through formation of an F-rich SEI.",
